3.KEYlogger

import tkinter as tk
def key_pressed(event):
 key = event.keysym
 label.config(text=f"Key pressed: {key}")
 with open("keys_log.txt", "a") as f:
 f.write(key + "\n")
# Create window
root = tk.Tk()
root.title("Keyboard Input Logger (Educational)")
root.geometry("400x200")
label = tk.Label(root, text="Press any key...", font=("Arial", 14))
label.pack(pady=40)
# Bind key press event
root.bind("<KeyPress>", key_pressed)
root.mainloop()
Steps to save and run
 Open Notepad / VS Code
 Paste the code
 Save as:
key_logger_demo.py
 Open Command Prompt / Terminal
 Navigate to the file location
 Run:
python key_logger_demo.py


Aim: To simulate a persistent Cross-Site Scripting (Stored XSS) attack.

Steps
Extract the DVWA ZIP file.
Copy the DVWA folder.
Paste it into:
C:\xampp\htdocs
Rename the folder to DVWA.
Open the DVWA config folder.
Rename the configuration file to config.inc.php as required by your lab setup.
Start Apache and MySQL from XAMPP.
Open Chrome and go to:
http://localhost/DVWA
Click Create / Reset Database.
Log in using your lab credentials.
Go to DVWA Security.
Set the security level to Low and click Submit.
Open XSS (Stored).
Enter your lab-approved harmless XSS test payload in the input field.
Click Sign Guestbook.
Reload the page.
Observe that the stored test script executes again.
This demonstrates Stored/Persistent XSS.
Practical 2 — SQL Injection in DVWA

Aim: To perform a SQL Injection attack.

Steps
Make sure XAMPP Apache and MySQL are running.
Open Chrome and go to:
http://localhost/DVWA
Log in to DVWA.
Go to DVWA Security.
Set the security level to Low and click Submit.
Open SQL Injection from the left-side menu.
First enter a normal value such as:
1
Click Submit.
Observe the normal database result.
For the lab demonstration, enter the SQL Injection test input specified in your practical sheet.
Click Submit.
Compare the result with the normal 1 query.
Explain that specially crafted input can change the intended SQL query logic.
The practical demonstrates the SQL Injection vulnerability in the intentionally vulnerable DVWA environment.
